<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.5.5: Examining an Equation in Two Variables and Its Graph, Part 2</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00138</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.5.5: Examining an Equation in Two Variables and Its Graph, Part 2</md:title>
    <md:uuid>58b2fb9c-86b7-4186-97e7-5376ccd165f7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A ceramic sugar bowl weighs 340 grams when empty. It is then filled with sugar. One tablespoon of sugar weighs 12.5 grams.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Write an equation to represent the relationship between the total weight of the bowl in grams, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>W</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and the tablespoons of sugar, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00004"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>W</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>340</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>340</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>740</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">When the sugar bowl is full, it weighs 740 grams. How many tablespoons of sugar can the bowl hold? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">32 tablespoons; <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>740</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>340</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>400</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>; Subtract 340 from both sides. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>32</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>; Divide both sides by 12.5.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00008">The graph represents the relationship between the number of tablespoons of sugar in the bowl and the total weight of the bowl.</para>
      
        <media alt="Graph of a line. Weight, grams. Sugar, tablespoons.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_07_12_01.svg"/>
        </media>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Which point on the graph could represent your answer to the previous question?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Point D, because its coordinates could be (32, 740).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">About how many tablespoons of sugar are in the bowl when the total weight is 600 grams?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">About 21 tablespoons. Looking at the graph, when the weight is 600, the line is about at 21.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
